  "text": "Allergies:\nPatient recorded as having No Known Allergies to Drugs\n\nChief Complaint:\nabdominal pain, ascites\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMr. ___ is a ___ year old deaf man with HCV cirrhosis complicated \nby ascites, esophageal varices, and encephalopathy who presents \nwith increasing abdominal girth.\n\nThe patient noted increasing abdominal girth x1 week. He also \nendorses epigastric pain x1 day, 'pressure' worse with standing, \nnon-radiating. He has 2 abdominal hernias which have worsened in \nsize over the past few weeks, for which his hepatologist \nrecently recommended he start wearing an abdominal binder. He \nrecently restarted his lactulose due to some worsening confusion \n(wife states he signs things funny, writes funny, and says \nthings that do not make sense occasionally), and has about 10 \nBMs per day.\n.\nWife also reports some cough productive of brown, non-bloody \nsputum a few days ago, and worsening DOE for several days. She \nnotes that he has been walking more slowly recently due to his \nbreathing when they were on vacation in CT over the psat three \ndays. Patient is audibly wheezy on exam, but cannot tell when \nthis started b/c he cannot hear it. He reports good urine output \nwithout dysuria, but patient reports frequency, urgency, \nnocturia, decreased and slowed stream for several months. He \nfirst started developing ascites 3 months ago, and has swelling \nin his lower extremities for one year which is not particular \nworse. Of note, he was hospitalized at ___ 2 \nmonths ago for volume overload, and reports he was diuresed \naggressively. No changes in his diuretic dose and has been \ncompliant with his medications. He has never had a paracentesis \nbefore. The pt decided to come to ___ ED because they are \nthinking about getting a second opinion from the ___ \nhere about treatment for his cirrhosis. Denies fevers, chills, \nnight sweats, nausea, vomiting, chest pain, hemetemesis, BRBPR. \nThinks he has gained weight.\n.\nReview of outside records reveals that patient had \nspironolactone decreased to 50 TID in ___ because his prior \ndose may have been causing dizziness and confusion. He had \ndecreased this dose to 50 daily when seen in ___ and did not \ncomplain of dizziness. At this visit he was advised to increase \nspironolactone to 75mg daily if had edema or increasing \nabdominal girth.\n.\nIn the ED, initial VS: 96.9 72 131/81 18 100% on RA. Labs \nsignificant for a Cre of 1.5. (baseline creatinine 1.0). Tbili \nof 2.0, and INR of 1.3. Patient underwent diagnostic \nparacentesis in ED which revealed no evidence of SBP. Abd U/S \nconfirmed moderate to large ascites, cirrhotic liver, patent \nportal veins, and splenomegaly.\n.\nCurrently, patient is audibly wheezy on exam, but denies \nabdominal pain. Feels abdomen is distended. His wife states he \nis fairly clear right now, but notes that his handwriting is \nworse when he was signing a medical release form in the room.\n.\nROS: Denies fever, chills, night sweats, headache, vision \nchanges, rhinorrhea, congestion, sore throat, chest pain, \nnausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, BRBPR, melena, \nhematochezia, dysuria, hematuria.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ yo M with hearing impairment, HCV cirrhosis c/b ascites who \npresents with increasing abdominal girth and acute renal \nfailure.\n.\n# Acute renal failure - ED resident spoke with PCP who stated no \nh/o renal dysfunction. Baseline creatinine unknown. Given new \nascites (over past three months), concern for HRS. However, Labs \nwere consistent with pre-renal etiology. His creatinine improved \nwith albumin. Diuretics were restarted with normal creatinine.  \nCr 1.1 at discharge. He will have labs checked as outpatient to \nbe faxed to PCP.\n.\n# SOB, epigastric pain - Low suspicion for ACS but with family \nh/o MI and HTN, but cardiac enzymes negative x3. CXR \nunremarkable. He was audibly wheezy on exam on admission, \nimproved with nebulizers. He was discharged with albuterol \ninhaler. He was continued on home prevacid.\n.\n# HCV cirrhosis c/b hepatic encephalopathy, variceal bleeding \nand ascites. MELD 16. Child ___ score 9. Pt is followed at \n___. His diuretics were held. Paracentesis done ___ \nwith results above. He was given lactulose and rifaximin with \nimprovement in his mental status. He was continued on \npropranolol. His diuretics were restarted at lower doses given \nhis history of dizziness with diuretics. The doses of his \nmedications were unclear and he was discharged on doses per \ndischarge meds below.\n.\nHe will have PCP and liver follow up as outpatient. Given that \nhe is established at ___, he would like to have his \nliver transplant work up there and was not set up in liver \nclinic at ___. Discharge summary was faxed to Dr. ___ \n___ (fax ___ and Dr. ___ ___.\n.\n# CONTACT: ___ (wife) ___ (video phone \nwith automatic translater)\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary:\nAcute kidney injury\nHepatic encephalopathy\n\nSecondary: \nHCV cirrhosis complicated by ascites, esophageal varices and \nhepatic encephalopathy",
